{"stream": "process_log", "ts": "2026-08-26T12:43:24.496809+00:00", "agent": "email_agent", "objectId": "534004984568", "run_id": "1e227230fc394ca3bed2298d281ee845", "step": 5, "event": "profile", "object_id": "534004984568", "email_id": "bharath.m@tekninjas.com", "first_name": "Bharath", "last_name": "Madupathi", "employee_id": "E00012", "job_title": "Head of Operations", "company": "Ramp", "industry": "FINANCIAL_SERVICES", "company_id": "C0012", "company_size_revenue": "4.7", "location": "", "linkedin_url": "", "phone": "+1 (469) 247-0345", "probability": 0, "email_status": "OPENED", "lead_context": "Ramp is a finance automation platform serving over 50,000 customers, having built a comprehensive suite of AI agents to automate manual financial workflows including expense policy enforcement, accounting classification, and invoice processing. In June 2026, Ramp launched its Applied AI Solutions offering, designed specifically to help larger enterprises deploy AI agents for complex financial workflows. Their Policy Agent, which uses LLMs to interpret and enforce expense policies written in natural language, demonstrates significant production deployment \u2014 including human-in-the-loop labeling sessions and careful context engineering. This puts Ramp squarely in the crosshairs of the regulatory moment the post describes: SR 26-2 carved agentic AI out of model risk scope in April 2026, but that didn't make the risk disappear \u2014 it moved the governance burden entirely onto the deploying organization. Because Ramp both operates agentic AI internally and sells agentic finance automation to tens of thousands of businesses \u2014 many of them regulated \u2014 it sits on both sides of the governance gap. Vendor LLMs are evaluated as third-party relationships, with the expectation that the bank \u2014 not the vendor \u2014 owns the outcome, which means Ramp's enterprise bank and fintech customers will increasingly scrutinize what audit trails and control evidence Ramp's agents actually produce. The natural opening question for a rep: *When your enterprise customers ask you to demonstrate that Ramp's AI agents are controlled and auditable end-to-end, what does that evidence currently look like \u2014 and who inside Ramp owns that answer?*", "company_website": "https://ramp.com", "company_about": "Finance operations platform offering corporate cards, expense management, and automation tools, serving tens of thousands of businesses.", "industry_group": "Corporate Cards & Spend Management", "missing_pointers": ["location", "linkedin_url"]}
