{"stream": "process_log", "ts": "2026-08-26T15:26:54.157711+00:00", "agent": "email_agent", "objectId": "534035230400", "run_id": "8ee1da99298a44d5adc09ba22d166a81", "step": 5, "event": "profile", "object_id": "534035230400", "email_id": "naveen.p@tekninjas.com", "first_name": "Kodanda", "last_name": "Naveen", "employee_id": "E00016", "job_title": "Managing Director", "company": "Innovaccer", "industry": "HEALTHCARE", "company_id": "C0016", "company_size_revenue": "11.5", "location": "", "linkedin_url": "", "phone": "+1 (469) 850-0314", "probability": 2, "email_status": "DELIVERED", "lead_context": "Innovaccer is a high-velocity healthcare AI platform company whose core product, Gravity, is a cloud-agnostic data and AI infrastructure layer that ingests and normalizes data from EHR systems, claims, pharmacy, labs, and social determinants of health, then surfaces it to a layer of autonomous agents. The company recently committed $250 million to expand this AI agent platform, which automates workflows across prior authorization, revenue cycle management, and population health. That scale of internal API surface area makes observability a genuine operational risk: Gravity already exposes pipeline observability covering throughput, latency, and failure hotspots, and the platform empowers developers to rapidly build and deploy agents using open APIs and self-service capabilities \u2014 meaning engineering teams are constantly shipping new FastAPI-style service endpoints under HIPAA and HITRUST compliance constraints. Most health systems and payers are still struggling to move AI beyond pilots and into enterprise workflows, despite heavy investment and mounting pressure to show ROI \u2014 and without structured request logging and correlated application logs across those APIs, debugging production failures in regulated environments becomes costly. Our FastAPI logging guide speaks directly to that gap. A good opening question: *When your engineering teams deploy new AI agents or API connectors on Gravity, what does your current production logging and request-tracing story look like \u2014 and how are you catching silent failures before they affect clinical or revenue workflows?*", "company_website": "https://innovaccer.com", "company_about": "Offers an AI-driven healthcare cloud that unifies data and deploys agents across clinical, operational, and financial workflows including population health, care management, and revenue cycle.", "industry_group": "Healthcare Data Platform / Population Health", "missing_pointers": ["location", "linkedin_url"]}
