{"stream": "process_log", "ts": "2026-08-26T20:11:32.010901+00:00", "agent": "email_agent", "objectId": "534035230400", "run_id": "ac2bfb04362a4023bfcda5ec2d9e3c28", "step": 5, "event": "profile", "object_id": "534035230400", "email_id": "naveen.p@tekninjas.com", "first_name": "Kodanda", "last_name": "Naveen", "employee_id": "E00016", "job_title": "Managing Director", "company": "", "industry": "", "company_id": "", "company_size_revenue": "", "location": "", "linkedin_url": "", "phone": "+1 (469) 850-0314", "probability": 2, "email_status": "DELIVERED", "lead_context": "Innovaccer is committing $250 million to expand its AI agent platform, which automates workflows across prior authorization, revenue cycle management, and population health. The foundation of this is Gravity, a cloud-agnostic data and AI infrastructure layer that ingests and normalizes data from EHR systems, claims, pharmacy, labs, remote monitoring, and social determinants of health. Named a Leader in the inaugural 2026 Gartner Magic Quadrant for Healthcare Provider Industry Cloud Platforms, the company is operating at a moment when most health systems and payers are still struggling to move AI beyond pilots and into enterprise workflows. This makes API observability acutely relevant: as Innovaccer deploys more agentic workflows across clinical and financial systems, logging is critical for understanding and debugging production APIs, and for APIs specifically, it breaks down into request logs \u2014 recording individual requests and responses \u2014 and application logs, which capture detailed messages from within the code. Bridging the gap between aggregated API metrics and request-level debugging is essential; when anomalies surface in metrics, teams need to drill down to the relevant requests to understand exactly what happened. For a platform serving health systems like Orlando Health, Adventist HealthCare, and Banner Health, the stakes of an uninspected API failure in a care workflow are high. Opening question: As Innovaccer's engineering teams scale the number of AI agents running across clinical and revenue cycle workflows, how are they currently handling request-level observability and log correlation when something breaks in production?", "company_website": "", "company_about": "", "industry_group": "", "missing_pointers": ["external_company_id", "industry", "company_size_revenue", "location", "linkedin_url"]}
